 "script": [
  {"id": "hook", "text": "The oceans just broke their all-time heat record. Why does that matter?"},
  {"id": "f1", "text": "Copernicus says September's average sea surface temperature, was twenty-one point oh five degrees Celsius. That is the highest for any September on record. The old mark, from twenty twenty-three, was twenty point nine two. NOAA reports a record anomaly for any month."},
  {"id": "f2", "text": "Copernicus ranks September the second warmest on record, one point six three degrees above pre-industrial levels. Zero point one one degrees cooler than September twenty twenty-three. A second month in a row above one point five."},
  {"id": "f3", "text": "Copernicus says El Niño is still strengthening in the Pacific, and could become one of the strongest on record. It expects the warm run to last into twenty twenty-seven. NOAA says this year is on track for the five warmest."},
  {"id": "cta", "text": "Will twenty twenty-seven set a new temperature record? Yes or no?"}
